{"t":1792852332,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"c0db12d9-7509-41c8-b9f7-ff12ab7cd52e","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-24T06:00:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 240600\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n200 AM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nA Coastal Flood Advisory has been issued for Annapolis.\nOtherwise, there have been no significant changes to the\nforecast thinking for the next seven days.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in many areas\n  tonight and Sunday night.\n\n- 2) Dry weather continues through next week along with \n  generally seasonable temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in \nmany areas tonight and Sunday night.\n\nWith a large area of Canadian high pressure building overhead\nthrough the weekend, conditions will likely become favorable \nfor radiational cooling (light to calm winds, mainly clear skies\nand a cool background airmass). At the moment, it is unclear\nwhich night will be the coldest, although tonight (Saturday\nnight) will have a slight advantage given the position of the \nhigh overhead.\n\nThe growing season for 2026 has ended along and west of the Blue\nRidge and Catoctin Mountains, but continues east of there. Any\nheadlines for frost/freeze conditions will only be issued where\nthe growing season remains active.\n\nFor tonight, more widespread cold (possible frost/freeze) \nconditions are possible as high pressure settles more directly \noverhead. This would bring the frost/freeze threat for most \nareas outside the major urban centers (downtown DC/Baltimore) and\nimmediate shore of the Chesapeake Bay. If the colder guidance \nverifies, then the growing season may end for more areas west of \nI-95 come early Sunday morning. The high will remain overhead \nthrough Monday morning, and the low-level airmass doesn't \nchange appreciably, so frost/freeze conditions will be possible \nagain Sunday night.\n\nDid not make appreciable changes to the previous min temperature\nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10 percentile and\nCanadian Regional).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues through next week along\nwith generally seasonable temperatures.\n\nCanadian high pressure will continue to prevail overhead over the \nnext several days as upper-level ridging builds in from the west. As \nthis ridging tracks east, a dry backdoor cold front will move \nthrough Tuesday and into Wednesday, with winds shifting \nnortheasterly in the wake of the front. With PWATs remaining low \nthrough this period, no precipitation is anticipated as this front \nmoves through.\n\nAs this surface high pressure continues on, temperatures remain \nseasonable, with highs in the 60s to low 70s. Overnight lows could \ngradually increase somewhat throughout the week as this high \npressure persists, with skies remaining mostly clear throughout the \nweek. A few models hint at this high pressure breaking down towards \nthe end of next week as a weak trough begins to form over the Ohio \nRiver Valley next Thursday. Regardless, expect continued dry and \nseasonable conditions to continue at least through the next \nseveral days as the current pattern remains largely unchanged.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail through early next week.\nThere are some hints that a slight uptick in low-level moisture\ncould lead to some patchy fog during the early morning hours\nthrough the weekend, though confidence in timing and extent of \nsub-VFR is low at this time. The NAM and related guidance is by\nfar the most aggressive with this outcome, but is an outlier.\n\nE to SE flow is expected today, with light to calm winds again \ntonight. Wind direction may be a bit variable, ranging from SE \nto NE as high pressure passes directly overhead Sunday.\n\nP6SM SKC as high pressure dominates early next week. A dry cold\nfront will cross the area late Monday night with winds shifting\nto N/NW Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nHigh pressure will build overhead this weekend. Winds will vary\na bit from northeast to southeast, but at light speeds less than\n10 knots. Winds stay below SCA criteria Monday.\n\nA dry backdoor cold front passing through could bring wind \ngusts near SCA criteria on Tuesday before diminishing again \novernight. Westerly winds Monday shift northerly Tuesday, then \neasterly Wednesday in the wake of the front.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nGenerally light but steady east to southeast winds are expected\nto keep water levels elevated through the weekend. When paired \nwith high astronomical tides due to the upcoming full moon on \nOctober 26, additional rounds of minor tidal flooding are \npossible. There has been a slight trend downward, so there is \nsome decrease in confidence for which locations reach minor \nflooding in forthcoming high tides. Guidance does show a more \nnotable decline in water levels by Monday when flow finally \nturns westerly.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Frost Advisory until 9 AM EDT this morning for MDZ004>006-008-\n     507-508.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 6 AM EDT early this morning for \n     MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/DHOF/LFR/KJP/SRT\nAVIATION...ADS/DHOF/LFR/SRT\nMARINE...ADS/DHOF/LFR/SRT\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...LWX\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792852252,"days":{"2026-10-24":{"hi":64,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-25":{"hi":68,"lo":41,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-26":{"hi":70,"lo":49,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-27":{"hi":68,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":66,"lo":48,"pop":1,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"h